{"authored" [{"question" "q-336932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-$\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/state.json" "file_sha256" "a546006f34867d121c7e5670d5d3de2f8e7d622cd5e0ed87e29b672e0d99d644" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-{9}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/state.json" "file_sha256" "a546006f34867d121c7e5670d5d3de2f8e7d622cd5e0ed87e29b672e0d99d644" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f338d77ee01746b9a0b348615cc7f4946f6911bf5686ee5f3f9adbf5eeafa1c3" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336932" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/state.json" "file_sha256" "a546006f34867d121c7e5670d5d3de2f8e7d622cd5e0ed87e29b672e0d99d644" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-336932" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\ln(9))^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/state.json" "file_sha256" "a546006f34867d121c7e5670d5d3de2f8e7d622cd5e0ed87e29b672e0d99d644" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-72333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/state.json" "file_sha256" "a546006f34867d121c7e5670d5d3de2f8e7d622cd5e0ed87e29b672e0d99d644" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-72333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{1/x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/state.json" "file_sha256" "a546006f34867d121c7e5670d5d3de2f8e7d622cd5e0ed87e29b672e0d99d644" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-72333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/a